WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.172
A regular expression can help us test whether text follows a particular structure.

00:00:05.172 --> 00:00:10.147
For the email example, we first handle missing or blank values.

00:00:10.147 --> 00:00:18.442
Then we test whether the cleaned email has a reasonable pattern: some text before the at sign, a domain after it,

00:00:18.442 --> 00:00:20.304
and a final domain ending.

00:00:20.821 --> 00:00:25.358
You do not need to memorize the full regular expression character by character.

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:30.143
Focus on what it is doing: it is checking the shape of the text.

00:00:30.143 --> 00:00:33.913
If the value matches, we label it valid shape.

00:00:33.913 --> 00:00:37.383
If it does not match, we label it for review.

00:00:37.383 --> 00:00:40.378
But remember what this does not prove.

00:00:40.378 --> 00:00:47.943
An address can have a perfect shape and still be nonexistent, inactive, or typed for the wrong person.

00:00:47.943 --> 00:00:51.953
Regular expressions are useful structural filters.

00:00:51.953 --> 00:00:54.354
They are not proof of real-world truth.
